00:07:34.236 --> 00:07:35.610
MICAH BAKER: Oh,
I can take that.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.010
So we got to announce
a strategic alliance

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.410
between Unity Technologies
and Google Cloud

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:43.080
to work on making
the development

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.010
of connected games faster and
easier for our developers.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:48.990
BRETT BIBBY: For
Unity, it means that we

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:51.300
can enable a whole new
generation of games

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:54.360
and experiences that
don't exist today.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.920
Unity has many creators
on its platforms.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:59.430
And this new alliance
will unlock a variety

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:01.980
of new innovative experiences.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.510
We're only successful when
our users are successful,

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:08.130
so this opens up a huge,
brand new, blue ocean

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:10.099
frontier of possibilities.

00:08:10.099 --> 00:08:12.640
MICAH BAKER: And you know, I
get this question a lot, though,

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:14.410
what is a connected game?

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:16.565
It might be good for us
to, for our audience,

00:08:16.565 --> 00:08:18.747
to define what we mean
by connected game.

00:08:18.747 --> 00:08:20.580
It might be straightforward
for some people,

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.460
but just to be clear, what
we mean by connected game

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.400
is games that are kind
of dynamic and living

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.950
where the developer
can update the content.

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:30.690
Could be a single
person game, or it

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.460
could be a multi-person game.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:35.130
And in the case of the
multiplayer type games,

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:37.950
they could be connected
by the developer

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:39.307
and also by other players.

00:08:39.307 --> 00:08:41.640
MELANIE WARRICK: So you were
talking about the developer

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.300
tools that we are working
as an alliance together with

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.840
and how we're doing
connected games.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:47.550
What are some of the
tools that are being used?

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.800
BRETT BIBBY: The way I think
of it is I kind of think

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.210
of it in three parts.

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:53.570
I mean, first, at
the core level,

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:56.257
you've got to have some
sort of networking.

00:08:56.257 --> 00:08:58.590
And this doesn't matter
whatever you're trying to build,

00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:00.180
if it's connected in
some way, eventually

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.160
you're going to have to
send some information

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.564
over some sort of network.

00:09:03.564 --> 00:09:04.980
And what's interesting
in games is

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.910
we actually identified three
critical core architectures

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:10.480
that are used in games.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.130
So for example, you have some
things that are like, you know,

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:16.650
multiplayer,
land-based experiences

00:09:16.650 --> 00:09:19.920
where a group of players are
running around and interacting

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:20.760
with each other.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.490
And that needs
one set of things.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:25.560
Those might be server
authoritative, for example.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.890
You have other kinds
of games like RTSes,

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:30.900
where you need lock step.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.660
In this frame, this
is what I'm doing,

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:33.910
and this is what you're doing.

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:36.630
And you have others that are
twitch, like fighting games,

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:39.270
where I'm going to punch you,
you're going to punch me.

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:42.000
But then, we're going
to have to figure out

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:44.160
a few frames later
what actually happened,

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.170
roll back, roll forward,
this kind of thing.

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:49.740
Now most networking solutions
try to solve this all in one.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:53.650
And what we've decided is that's
actually not the right way

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:54.150
to do it.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:56.525
Instead of being jack of all
trades, we need to split up.

00:09:56.525 --> 00:09:58.440
So just at the core
level, it's really

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.760
about implementing these three
networking architectures that,

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:06.030
really, out of the box,
not only perform well,

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:08.820
but they're set up
in a way that makes

00:10:08.820 --> 00:10:11.670
these kinds of
experiences easy to build,

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:13.590
which means the code,
everything else on top

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.030
of that is easy to build.

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:16.890
Now, that's just a
foundational piece.

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:18.930
And the next thing you
need is you actually

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:21.520
need to build an
experience on top of this.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.130
And Unity and Google
are going to make

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:27.450
that super seamless to not only
build, but deploy your game.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:29.910
And we demonstrated this
last week at Unite Berlin.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:34.354
And I almost think it got a
little lost in the fanfare.

00:10:34.354 --> 00:10:36.020
People didn't really
know what they saw.

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:39.125
But what we showed was
the strategic alliance

00:10:39.125 --> 00:10:40.500
between Google
and Unity is going

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.110
to make that super seamless
to go from developing

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:47.250
on my local machine, working and
play testing in a small group,

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:49.590
and then spilling
over into the Cloud.

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:51.870
And then when I'm actually
ready to launch and live

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:54.330
operate this game,
that's going to be

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:56.127
a huge change in the
number of players

00:10:56.127 --> 00:10:57.210
that are going to come in.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:10:59.990
And what we demonstrated
at Unite Berlin was

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:01.980
there's no difference
between these.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:06.150
We can seamlessly scale
from a local machine

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:10.800
to a small cloud experience
connecting our testers together

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:13.350
to then a huge
number of players.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:17.100
And we showed 1.5
million player capacity,

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.920
more than 60,000
servers being spun up

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:21.610
in less than three minutes.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:23.130
So that's crazy.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:25.590
That's something that
hasn't been possible before.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:28.860
And Google plus Unity
are uniquely positioned

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:30.480
to actually solve that.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.440
MARK MANDEL: So this
sounds pretty amazing.

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:34.982
Micah, what does this mean for
Google Cloud Platform, though?

00:11:34.982 --> 00:11:36.856
MICAH BAKER: Well, so
one of the great things

00:11:36.856 --> 00:11:39.090
about this strategic alliance
is that Unity already

00:11:39.090 --> 00:11:42.360
provides a leading
solution for developers

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:44.549
to work on their game vision.

00:11:44.549 --> 00:11:46.590
They have a big dream of
what they want to build,

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:47.520
but they don't want
to have to mess

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.417
with all the complexity
of the tooling

00:11:49.417 --> 00:11:51.500
and worrying about all the
platforms and so forth.

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:55.410
So Unity does this great job of
democratizing game development.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:57.510
And what Google Cloud is
bringing to this picture

00:11:57.510 --> 00:12:00.870
is democratizing access to
the cloud infrastructure.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:03.931
So while Unity could develop
a whole bunch of things

00:12:03.931 --> 00:12:06.180
on their own, they're going
to be able to do it faster

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:07.620
by collaborating
with Google Cloud

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:10.670
to do the infrastructure work,
which is our core expertise.

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:14.550
And so combined, we're going
to produce managed services

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:18.310
for these same developers to
do these one-click deployments

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:20.700
of multiplayer games
straight into the cloud.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.670
So all these things will
run on Google Cloud,

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:24.920
but the developers won't
have to know what that means.

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:27.790
They won't have to know, what
is that product I'm using?

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:30.810
They just need to know, I
got this scaled up globally,

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:32.880
and it runs my game,
and I'm not going

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.820
to be a victim of my
success, which is a danger.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:38.342
Maybe it's very popular
when your game launches,

00:12:38.342 --> 00:12:40.800
and your infrastructure crumbles
because it wasn't actually

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:41.540
planned well.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:43.230
We'll make sure that
that's always planned well.

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:44.490
BRETT BIBBY: And
this is a super--

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:46.590
I mean, just building on that,
that's a super hard problem

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:47.230
to solve.

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:51.070
I mean, I don't know that it's
actually been solved before.

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:52.620
People have solved
individual pieces

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:55.260
and optimized for
themselves on it.

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:58.320
Even the most experienced
game developers in the world

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:02.890
have suffered from day one
outages or peak issues.

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:08.130
And so I think Google's ability
to seamlessly scale and Unity's

00:13:08.130 --> 00:13:10.710
ability to build the
front end experience,

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:13.050
it is a pretty
interesting combination

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:14.370
when you put it together.

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:15.210
MARK MANDEL: Micah,
does this mean

00:13:15.210 --> 00:13:17.280
that Unity developers
are going to get access

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.200
to specific Google
Cloud products?

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:22.020
Or do we have any plans on
what that might look like?

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.020
MICAH BAKER: Yeah, it's
an interesting question.

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:26.580
Because we really want
to walk this line of not

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:28.080
adding complexity
to the development

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.070
environment and process.

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:34.320
But we do want to provide
access to innovative Cloud

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:38.430
technologies that could actually
enable new types of gameplay.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:41.220
For example, we have a
product called Cloud Spanner,

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:44.430
which enables a global
relational database

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:45.840
for any developer.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.314
But if I just said,
hey, developer, here's

00:13:48.314 --> 00:13:49.980
Cloud Spanner, they
wouldn't necessarily

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:51.300
know what that even means.

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:54.180
But if I said, hey, do you want
a global relational database

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:57.617
for game save data or game
state data or player data,

00:13:57.617 --> 00:13:59.200
they're going to
know what that means.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:00.810
So what we will be
doing is offering

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:03.780
these kind of prepackaged
solutions to the developers

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:05.250
through the Unity
package manager,

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.950
and they're going to be able to
access the solution that they

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:10.140
want without having to
worry about which product

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:12.834
or products are actually doing
the work behind the scenes.

00:14:12.834 --> 00:14:14.250
MELANIE WARRICK:
Can you talk more

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:16.669
about the plans for the future
of this strategic alliance?

00:14:16.669 --> 00:14:18.960
You were talking a little
bit about the infrastructure,

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:20.418
the core tools that
you're sharing.

00:14:20.418 --> 00:14:21.846
What the next steps look like.

00:14:21.846 --> 00:14:23.220
BRETT BIBBY: Well,
the next steps

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:27.390
are, obviously, getting
our two groups together.

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:29.610
And we're doing that
here at a Hack Week,

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.530
already starting
the steps on that.

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:33.600
I think, Mark, you've
got a project that's

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:37.260
starting those early steps that
you can talk about where we're

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:38.760
munging some stuff
together and just

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:41.100
really understanding
and coming together,

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.732
like the sets of
problems we solve,

00:14:42.732 --> 00:14:44.190
the sets of problems
Google solves,

00:14:44.190 --> 00:14:46.590
and how we can do that
effectively together.

00:14:46.590 --> 00:14:48.810
But I think we do
have a long range

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:51.090
vision of it's just seamless.

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:52.350
It's stepwise.

00:14:52.350 --> 00:14:54.960
It's easy for people to adopt.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:56.460
They know what
they're going to get.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.370
They can put those
blocks together

00:14:59.370 --> 00:15:02.430
and focus on actually
building great experiences

00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:04.960
and not worrying about
the other bits and pieces.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.900
So I'd say in the
short term, it's

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:08.880
understanding the
landscape of all the things

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:10.020
that we need to do.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:16.610
In the long term vision is
that it's just dead simple

00:15:16.610 --> 00:15:22.820
to start, build, deploy,
and run your experiences.

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:24.454
And that's what
we're committed to.

00:15:24.454 --> 00:15:26.620
And we're going to do
whatever it takes between here

00:15:26.620 --> 00:15:27.629
and there to get there.

00:15:27.629 --> 00:15:29.920
MARK MANDEL: And Brett, it's
also worth noting I think,

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:32.010
you're moving all your core
infrastructure to Google Cloud

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:32.710
Platform as well?

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:33.250
BRETT BIBBY: That's right.

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:34.124
You know, it's funny.

00:15:34.124 --> 00:15:35.890
I mean, Unity is
founded by people

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:38.050
from three different
countries here in Denmark.

00:15:38.050 --> 00:15:41.980
And our first employee
was located in Iceland,

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:44.990
our second was in Germany,
our third was in Lithuania.

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:47.940
So from day one, we've been
sort of a connected company.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:51.340
And we built all of that
just, as many others have,

00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:54.190
on just the G Suite,
the Google Platform.

00:15:54.190 --> 00:15:57.160
And we've been using
all of your applications

00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:58.840
for more than a decade.

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:01.060
And along the way,
we've also started

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:04.570
using it for various other
Cloud services that you offer.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:08.620
And we're super excited to
move all of our infrastructure

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:12.440
over to the Google tech, because
we've had nothing but success,

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:13.370
and it's what we know.

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:15.766
And it's sort of, I think,
when we look at the stuff,

00:16:15.766 --> 00:16:17.890
it implements things and
approaches things in a way

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:22.330
that we get and that
is, I think, accessible.

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:25.990
We have this saying at Unity,
accessible yet uncompromising.

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:27.580
It's really easy to get into.

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:29.555
But then when you need
to go deep, it's there.

00:16:29.555 --> 00:16:30.430
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:31.810
And the question I
keep getting is, well,

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:33.643
when I'm here at Hack
Week, which is lovely,

00:16:33.643 --> 00:16:36.100
if I'm a Unity developer,
what does this migration mean

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:36.730
for me?

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:38.167
What am I getting out of this?

00:16:38.167 --> 00:16:40.000
MICAH BAKER: So if
you're a Unity developer,

00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:43.090
and you want to think about
the infrastructure migration,

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:46.420
you really should think
about your infrastructure

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:50.249
that you use, and Unity will
perform as well or better

00:16:50.249 --> 00:16:51.040
than it did before.

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:53.110
But it should be kind of
transparent to the Unity

00:16:53.110 --> 00:16:54.200
developer.

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:56.740
But it also means that
the future services

00:16:56.740 --> 00:16:59.440
that Unity launches will be
launching on Google Cloud,

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:01.510
and we'll help with the
architecting of that

00:17:01.510 --> 00:17:03.790
and getting those optimized
to run on Google Cloud.

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:06.280
That also includes things
like our recently announced

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.170
open source matchmaking
project, which

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:10.900
will be not only launched
as an open source project,

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:14.829
but also become a managed
service on the Unity platform

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:17.170
so that a developer
could easily integrate

00:17:17.170 --> 00:17:19.690
a matchmaker into their
game without having

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:22.579
to know how to build an entire
matchmaker from scratch.

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:25.750
We're just getting started with
these multiplayer technologies,

00:17:25.750 --> 00:17:27.730
and there will be
more services to come.

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:29.530
And they'll all be
built on Google Cloud.

00:17:29.530 --> 00:17:30.980
MELANIE WARRICK: Mark,
you were talking about how

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.440
you guys are at Hack Week.

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:34.900
What are some of the
particularly awesome things

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:37.667
you've been seeing
hacked on at Hack Week?

00:17:37.667 --> 00:17:39.250
MARK MANDEL: So it's
been pretty good.

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:42.100
I've been working on a
project with a group of Unity

00:17:42.100 --> 00:17:42.750
employees.

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:45.250
There's a bunch of us working
on taking an existing demo

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:46.890
and turning it into
a multiplayer demo.

00:17:46.890 --> 00:17:49.390
So I've been working with the
teams that are working on some

00:17:49.390 --> 00:17:50.431
of the multiplayer stuff.

00:17:50.431 --> 00:17:52.540
We're hacking on some new
things, some things that

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:54.940
are coming, and just trying to
mung all the things together.

00:17:54.940 --> 00:17:56.023
It's been really exciting.

00:17:56.023 --> 00:17:58.240
But I've seen people
with VR helmets on.

00:17:58.240 --> 00:18:00.110
And I've seen people
with, I don't know,

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:02.330
it looks like network
gear floating around.

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:03.850
Brett, I'm sure you've seen
a whole bunch of stuff here.

00:18:03.850 --> 00:18:05.641
You've been practically
running this thing.

00:18:05.641 --> 00:18:06.220
Haven't you?

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:06.928
BRETT BIBBY: Yes.

00:18:06.928 --> 00:18:10.790
And what's funny is that
I'm always surprised.

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:14.134
I mean, this will be our
13th or 14th Hack Week,

00:18:14.134 --> 00:18:14.800
and I'm always--

00:18:14.800 --> 00:18:17.860
I saw one of our senior
graphics engineers

00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:20.860
with a sewing machine late
into the night the other night.

00:18:20.860 --> 00:18:23.440
So obviously, there's some
surprise waiting for us.

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:25.840
But they've built some
sort of AR experience

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:29.410
that uses clothing props
and every other thing you

00:18:29.410 --> 00:18:30.070
can imagine.

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:30.550
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:32.424
Tell us a bit of the
background on Hack Week.

00:18:32.424 --> 00:18:33.341
What's this all about?

00:18:33.341 --> 00:18:35.715
BRETT BIBBY: We have more than
500 talented women and men

00:18:35.715 --> 00:18:37.510
building the core Unity
product, and we're

00:18:37.510 --> 00:18:38.890
distributed across the globe.

00:18:38.890 --> 00:18:41.500
We're actually located
in more than 30 cities,

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:43.450
about 19 countries.

00:18:43.450 --> 00:18:46.330
And functionally, it's a chance
for us to gather in one place

00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:47.117
once a year.

00:18:47.117 --> 00:18:48.700
But it's actually
much more than that.

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:50.710
Most of the innovations
and breakthroughs

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:52.660
we have brought to
the Unity product

00:18:52.660 --> 00:18:54.220
have actually come
out of Hack Weeks.

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:55.150
Why?

00:18:55.150 --> 00:18:58.570
Well, it's actually because you
mix a diverse set of people,

00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:01.210
diverse set of skills,
domain knowledge,

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:03.820
all collaborating in one place,
and you simmer it on high

00:19:03.820 --> 00:19:05.590
for a week and magic happens.

00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:07.600
So this is great.

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:09.730
And this year, we
have a special guest.

00:19:09.730 --> 00:19:12.640
So we have Google with us
this year, which is fantastic.

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:15.640
And we're also joined by
almost 50 women engineers

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:18.760
from around the world as part of
our Women in Gaming initiative.

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:21.460
And they're hacking
alongside of this as well.

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:26.020
So it's just great the crazy
mix of people and the diversity

00:19:26.020 --> 00:19:27.100
that we're getting.

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:28.017
It's actually magical.

00:19:28.017 --> 00:19:30.308
MARK MANDEL: Well, thank you
very much for inviting us.

00:19:30.308 --> 00:19:32.080
I've been having a
really good time.

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:33.130
BRETT BIBBY: I guess
we should ask you.

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:34.930
I mean, we kind of
reversed the question,

00:19:34.930 --> 00:19:37.090
I guess, but like, what
do you think of Hack Week?

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:40.600
I mean, I've only actually
ever been at a Unity

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:41.920
internal Hack Week.

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:43.960
I mean, like, what
do you make of it?

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:44.710
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:45.710
