00:19:49.822 --> 00:19:51.280
But it's been really
great to get--

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:53.750
I mean, I only know like
a little part of it.

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:55.570
And so it's been really great to
get the different perspectives

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:58.028
and see how, basically, the
people who live in the trenches

00:19:58.028 --> 00:20:00.087
with this stuff, how
they build things.

00:20:00.087 --> 00:20:02.420
And also, just everyone's
really lovely, which is great.

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:04.060
It's been an absolute
pleasure hanging out here.

00:20:04.060 --> 00:20:05.980
I mean, where we are
physically is gorgeous,

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:07.120
so can't complain too much.

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:07.570
MICAH BAKER: Yeah.

00:20:07.570 --> 00:20:08.790
It's a fantastic setting.

00:20:08.790 --> 00:20:12.250
I'm also really impressed at
the ambitions of the people

00:20:12.250 --> 00:20:13.040
here at Hack Week.

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:15.165
And when you look at the
different projects they're

00:20:15.165 --> 00:20:17.950
taking on, people are not
going for just something

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:18.910
I could do in my sleep.

00:20:18.910 --> 00:20:21.280
They're going for hard
problems, and it's things

00:20:21.280 --> 00:20:22.900
that they're not
currently working on.

00:20:22.900 --> 00:20:25.300
So I think it's really
focused on the right things

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:27.370
to generate those
innovations that only come

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:29.770
from getting away from
your normal routines

00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:31.270
and getting with
new people, and you

00:20:31.270 --> 00:20:32.680
get a whole different dynamic.

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:34.410
MARK MANDEL: I love the
fact, as well, we got told,

00:20:34.410 --> 00:20:35.560
if you're here, and
I know you're here

00:20:35.560 --> 00:20:37.610
with all your teams, but
meetings are optional.

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:39.318
I wish I had meetings
are optional weeks.

00:20:39.318 --> 00:20:40.130
That'd be great.

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:41.680
BRETT BIBBY: Well, the
irony there is, of course,

00:20:41.680 --> 00:20:43.096
I'm the one that
said that, right?

00:20:43.096 --> 00:20:46.090
And then I had a meeting,
and everybody showed up just

00:20:46.090 --> 00:20:47.770
to say, I'm not
coming to this meeting

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:49.950
because it was optional.

00:20:49.950 --> 00:20:51.017
So it's fantastic.

00:20:51.017 --> 00:20:52.850
MELANIE WARRICK: It
sounds like a good time.

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:54.790
And I like how you just
turned the tables on Mark

00:20:54.790 --> 00:20:55.600
with the question.

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:57.610
So anything else
that you guys wanted

00:20:57.610 --> 00:21:00.130
to cover around our
strategic alliance in terms

00:21:00.130 --> 00:21:02.170
of where things
are at with Unity,

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:04.720
with GCP, with the Hack Week?

00:21:04.720 --> 00:21:07.180
MICAH BAKER: I mean, there's
a lot of feedback coming in.

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:09.580
There's lots of resources I
guess we could talk about.

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:10.120
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:21:10.120 --> 00:21:11.470
Let's talk about resources.

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:13.011
MICAH BAKER: There's
currently a blog

00:21:13.011 --> 00:21:16.030
posting that you can look
at on blogs@Unity3D.com,

00:21:16.030 --> 00:21:20.170
which is currently taking
questions and responding

00:21:20.170 --> 00:21:20.670
to things.

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:22.750
So if people are
interested, definitely

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:24.660
suggest heading
over to Unity3D.com

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:27.680
and looking around at the
connected games information.

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:29.980
BRETT BIBBY: So we have
the privilege actually of--

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:32.260
because of our
success, we are also

00:21:32.260 --> 00:21:34.900
early in the labs with people.

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:36.610
We have pretty
good view of what's

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:39.070
happening in the next year,
two years, three years.

00:21:39.070 --> 00:21:41.470
And I like, you know, you
take where you are today,

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:43.470
where you think you're
going to be in two years,

00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:46.370
and then you also look
out 10, 15, 20 years.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:49.690
And you can kind of get a pretty
good bearing on what's what.

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:51.190
And if you think
about it, could you

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:54.440
imagine 20 years from
now or 30 years from now

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:58.090
not having some
form of connectivity

00:21:58.090 --> 00:22:00.762
when experiencing content?

00:22:00.762 --> 00:22:02.470
I mean, it doesn't
mean that everything's

00:22:02.470 --> 00:22:05.230
going to be a massive
multiplayer game

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:06.560
or anything like that.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:09.790
But it's a certainty
that everything

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:11.690
is becoming more
and more connected,

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:13.240
and that there's value in that.

00:22:13.240 --> 00:22:15.700
And from a gameplay
perspective, we

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:21.684
know that content is the most
expensive way to build value.

00:22:21.684 --> 00:22:24.100
And in the old days, it was
kind of like, well, we're just

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:27.307
going to throw 30 levels,
50 levels, 100 levels,

00:22:27.307 --> 00:22:29.890
and if you wanted to, say, do a
bunch of different mini games,

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:32.223
there's a huge amount of
engineering cost in doing that.

00:22:32.223 --> 00:22:34.760
So what we had to do
is make that content.

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:37.330
But as we move from standard
definition, high definition,

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:40.730
ultra high definition, the
scene complexity is growing,

00:22:40.730 --> 00:22:42.970
we need something else,
and we need some way

00:22:42.970 --> 00:22:44.090
to augment that gameplay.

00:22:44.090 --> 00:22:46.540
And fortunately for
us, human beings

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:49.570
make pretty good artificial
intelligence for games.

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:52.810
And so when you
mix humans together

00:22:52.810 --> 00:22:56.350
collaborating or competing
in online environments,

00:22:56.350 --> 00:22:59.440
when you bring dynamic
elements to stuff,

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:04.660
you're able to create a very
live dynamic experience that

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:05.750
is super engaging.

00:23:05.750 --> 00:23:09.700
And I think-- so connected
games is so crucial.

00:23:09.700 --> 00:23:12.470
Getting out and going to
learn about this stuff

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:15.109
is really critical for
people's success going forward.

00:23:15.109 --> 00:23:17.650
MELANIE WARRICK: Brett, I'm glad
you mentioned about content,

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:21.910
because I've noticed more and
more the media is becoming more

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:24.940
merged, media like film,
television, and concepts

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:26.740
are getting more and
more merged in gaming.

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:28.510
Do you see that
continuing to be,

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:31.740
these spaces that really
merge together, especially

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:33.659
as the technology
continues to advance?

00:23:33.659 --> 00:23:34.450
BRETT BIBBY: Right.

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:37.030
Well, in terms of
merging together,

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:39.100
there's a lot of different
viewpoints on this.

00:23:39.100 --> 00:23:41.890
I think that when
I imagine playing

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:44.680
a game in my living room
in augmented reality, yeah,

00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:45.640
that's pretty cool.

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:47.380
Do I like the idea
of walking down

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:52.330
the street in augmented reality
and living in something that's

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:53.740
marked up by other people?

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:55.660
You know, it sounds
kind of interesting,

00:23:55.660 --> 00:23:58.270
but I think that I'm not
sure that the world is

00:23:58.270 --> 00:24:00.340
ready for complete immersion.

00:24:00.340 --> 00:24:03.670
I think that's what's driving
it towards the content itself.

00:24:03.670 --> 00:24:06.010
I think making the
content more alive,

00:24:06.010 --> 00:24:10.480
the behavior behind the content
is really, really critical.

00:24:10.480 --> 00:24:14.590
But I don't know that the
mixing, the mash up of content

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:17.500
is something that's ready for
the prime time out in the world

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:18.330
today.

