00:25:37.120 --> 00:25:38.560
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes,
and this week's question

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:40.720
is, how do I report
errors to Stackdriver

00:25:40.720 --> 00:25:42.160
from a Cloud Function?

00:25:42.160 --> 00:25:43.580
MARK MANDEL: So this is
pretty straightforward,

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:44.210
which is kind of nice.

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:46.240
You're just going to
need to know where it is.

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:49.000
You can essentially emit an
error from a Cloud Function

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:52.530
to Stackdriver Error
Reporting by simply--

00:25:52.530 --> 00:25:54.160
I love things that say simply--

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:56.950
by simply logging an
error object to a console.

00:25:56.950 --> 00:25:58.870
So basically,
console.error, and then

00:25:58.870 --> 00:26:00.490
passing in the error message.

00:26:00.490 --> 00:26:01.582
It's really that simple.

00:26:01.582 --> 00:26:03.040
It may not necessarily
be something

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:04.498
that's immediately
picked up if you

00:26:04.498 --> 00:26:06.950
want to get the specific
details of an error.

00:26:06.950 --> 00:26:08.750
So doing it this way
is important inside

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:10.600
like your try
catches, and basically

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:14.940
how you handle your errors
inside of your Node.js type

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:15.580
applications.

00:26:15.580 --> 00:26:17.395
Well, inside your Cloud
Functions at least.

00:26:17.395 --> 00:26:19.270
It's worth noting that
the following will not

00:26:19.270 --> 00:26:21.603
emit a Stackdriver error--
throwing something that's not

00:26:21.603 --> 00:26:23.680
an error object, for
example, logging an error

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:26.260
object at the info level,
logging something that's

00:26:26.260 --> 00:26:29.260
not an error per se either,
and that kind of stuff.

00:26:29.260 --> 00:26:31.750
So make sure you use
console.error, and then pass

00:26:31.750 --> 00:26:33.400
in your error message, and
you should be good to go.

00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:33.730
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:35.396
And they have a nice
little bit here too

00:26:35.396 --> 00:26:38.920
that talks about how if you're
not sure it's an error object,

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:41.280
that they show you basically
how you can check it.

